The current version only uses the lookup table for message numbers below 1024. Any larger number, such as manufacturer-specific messages (0xFF00 and up) or unknown messages, falls back to a linear scan over every profile entry. That scan usually ends in a miss, so it is the worst case.

This rewrite extends the table to all 65,536 possible message numbers, so every lookup is one indexed load. The table is 128 KB and is filled once.

Behavior is unchanged:
- The table is filled from the last entry to the first, so when two entries share a number, the first one still wins, as in the linear scan.
- The `ok` fallback to the linear scan is kept for the case where the profile has too many entries to fit the 16-bit index.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    enum { LUT_SIZE = 65536 };
    struct Lut
    {
        FIT_SINT16 idx[LUT_SIZE];
        bool ok;
        Lut() : ok(MESGS < 32767)
        {
            for (int k = 0; k < LUT_SIZE; k++)
                idx[k] = -1;
            if (!ok)
                return;
            for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            {
                FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
                idx[n] = (FIT_SINT16)i;
            }
        }
    };
    static const Lut lut;

    if (lut.ok)
    {
        int i = lut.idx[num];
        return (i >= 0) ? &mesgs[i] : NULL;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
    {
        if (mesgs[i].num == num)
            return &mesgs[i];
    }

    return NULL;
}
```